### `announcement_day_trade.py`

Buys 0DTE calls on SP500 rebalancing announcement Friday (top 4 by volume with tiered premium: $100k/$75k/$50k/$25k), then sells 50% of contracts as short +10% one-week calls.

**Output:** `sp500_announcement_day_trade[_strikeN]_{YYYYMMDD}.xlsx`

| Sheet | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| Hold Short Calls to Exp w DH | Short calls held to expiry, first delta hedge Monday close |
| Close Short Calls Monday AM | Short calls closed at 10am Monday, no delta hedge |
| All Data | Both variants stacked (`Type` column) |
| Notes | Strategy descriptions |
| Summary | Native Excel pivot (PnL by Type / date / symbol) |

**Key config:** `DAYS_BEFORE=0`, `DAYS_AFTER=1`, `STRIKE_IDX=1` (2nd OTM call), `AMOUNT_OF_SHORT_TO_SELL=0.5`

**Refactored into shared functions** (one code path for the normal run and the strike sweep): `build_zero_dte_call_df(strike_idx)`, `add_0dte_pnl(zdf, exit_cache)`, `build_one_week_hold(zdf)`, `build_one_week_monday(zdf)`, `scale_short(...)`, `merge_variant(...)`, `run_hold_to_exp(strike_idx)`, and `export_announcement_excel(combined, combined_close_monday_am, suffix="")`. The data source is the on-disk store (`load_symbol_dates`), loaded one symbol at a time. A backup of the pre-refactor script is kept at `announcement_day_trade_old.py`. See **Strike-Index Sweep** below.

### `candidates_buying_vol.py`

Buys ATM calls on SP500 candidates, hedges with short SPY calls (vega-neutral). Seven strategy variants testing different entry timings and option tenors. Tags stocks with earnings in the trade window via `had_earnings` column (filter in Excel).

**Output:** `buy_sp500_candidate_vol_{YYYYMMDD}.xlsx`

| Sheet | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| Long Vol | Eff date 9:35am → 3:30pm same day, ~66-day options |
| Long Vol T-1 Initiation | T-1 3:30pm → eff 3:30pm, ~66-day options |
| Long Vol T-3 Initiation | T-3 3:30pm → eff 3:30pm, ~66-day options |
| Long Vol T-5 Initiation | T-5 3:30pm → eff 3:30pm, ~66-day options |
| Long Vol Hold Thru Monday | Eff 9:35am → next trading day 10:00am, ~66-day options |
| Long Vol T-1 Init Closer Dated | T-1 3:30pm → eff 3:30pm, ~5-day options |

**Key config:** `MAX_LOOKBACK=5`, `DAYS_AFTER=1`, `hedge_symbol="SPY"`, `SNS_BAND=2.0` (ATM band loaded per leg), trade_vega=5000 per candidate

**Data:** store-backed. Each leg loads via the two-phase, drift-safe `load_leg_frame()` — entry-date ATM band (`|sns| <= SNS_BAND`) to resolve the strike, then that exact strike on the exit date — one leg at a time, so peak memory is a single leg. The full surface stays on disk, so widening `SNS_BAND` is a re-read, not a re-pull.

**Configuration flags:**

| Flag | Default | Description |
|------|---------|-------------|
| `USE_TOP_FROM_LAST_CYCLE` | `False` | Only trades the ~5 top symbols from the prior cycle (defined in `TOP_SYMBOLS_BY_CYCLE` in `sp500_helpers.py`). Bypasses illiquid filter. Output file gets `_top_from_last` suffix. |
| `VEGA_FLAT` | `True` | SPY hedge sized by raw vega sum instead of vol-weighted vega. Removes the ATM vol weighting that gives higher-vol names more hedge weight. Output file gets `_vega_flat` suffix. |

### `candidates_buying_vol_puts.py`

Put twin of `candidates_buying_vol.py`, kept as a separate script so the calls version stays untouched. Buys **-1 sns puts** on candidates (`TARGET_SNS = -1.0`) and hedges with **short SPY -1 sns puts** (vega-neutral, same `VEGA_FLAT` logic). Same 6 timing variants, same $5k vega/name, same flags (`USE_TOP_FROM_LAST_CYCLE`, `VEGA_FLAT`).

**Output:** `buy_sp500_candidate_put_vol[_top_from_last][_vega_flat]_{YYYYMMDD}.xlsx` — tabs named "Put Vol", "Put Vol T-1 Initiation", etc., same All Data / Descriptions / Summary pivot layout.

**Data:** cp-specific store files `opt_store/{SYMBOL}/{date}__Put.parquet` (the shared calls-only `{date}.parquet` files are never touched). Fill is incremental/resumable like the calls script. Note: for dates the DB doesn't already hold, liberator returns both cps unfiltered, so some `__Put` files contain call rows — harmless, `leg` filters by cp at selection time.

**Kernel-collision-safe naming** (both scripts can live in one kernel): result vars are `combined_put*`; helpers that differ are renamed (`_run_candidate_put_backtest`, `_run_spy_put_hedge`, `PUT_STRATEGIES`, `clear_put_caches`). The calls script and `announcement_day_trade.py` share a `combined` variable name — whichever ran last wins, so re-run the relevant strategy cells before any export.

**Finding (8/31/26, Zach):** the ATM call version (`candidates_buying_vol.py`) performs better than the -1 sns put version — the At The Money vol trade looks like the better trade.

