Takeaways (small-N caveat: 10 obs, 6 distinct FED days):
- Median excess +39 bps, but mean only +7 bps -- dragged down by APO (-201) and VRT (-110).
- 7 of 10 added names outperformed SPY 2pm -> close on their FED day.
- Dec 2024 FOMC (hawkish cut) was the only meeting where BOTH same-cycle adds underperformed (APO, WDAY).
- The 14:00 anchor isn't tied to the FOMC decision time (2pm ET decision was historical; current 2026 schedule is 2:00pm decision + 2:30pm presser, so 2pm captures decision-instant onward). If you want to isolate post-presser drift, repeat with a 14:30 anchor.

### `short_candidates_t1.py`

Standalone (Bloomberg only via `bloomberg_helpers.my_bdh`, raw `adjust='-'`, cached; no liberator). Reads `ANNOUNCEMENT_CYCLES` / `INDIVIDUAL_ADDS` out of `sp500_helpers.py` via AST so it does not trigger the liberator import chain. Requested by Matt Courter 9/8/26.

**Trade:** on each quarterly announcement Friday, short the top-10 `CUR_MKT_CAP` names from that cycle's candidate list at the close, long SPY for the same gross notional. Because S&P publishes after the close, the eventual add is unknown and stays in the basket (variant A, tradeable). Variant C enters at the T+1 open ex the selected add(s) and exits at the T+1 close.

- Ranking (`RANK_BY`): `'mkt_cap'` = top-N by `CUR_MKT_CAP` on the annc date; `'atm_vol'` (current default) = top-N by announcement-Friday 0DTE `atmVol` (earliest expiry, first snap after 15:30, pool of 10 by atmVol -- same as `announcement_day_trade.build_zero_dte_call_df`) with a **different volume filter** (Zach, 9/8/26): total 0DTE call volume across ALL strikes (last cumulative `prtVolume` per strike, summed) must be >= 2,000 contracts. SpiderRock's `-99` in `prtVolume` is a NaN sentinel and is ignored; the announcement-day trade's legacy metric (mean `prtVolume` over OTM strikes after 15:40, `> 100`) leaves the -99s in, which biases against names with many untraded strikes -- it is kept in the CSV as `legacy_mean_otm_vol`. The ranking is computed ONCE in the shared kernel by `sp500_helpers.build_atm_vol_ranking()` (reads the `opt_store/` Friday files, no liberator/Snowflake) and saved to `atm_vol_ranking.csv`; the backtest script reads the CSV. Regenerate the CSV after filling a new cycle. With the legacy filter the top 4 per cycle matched the kernel's `zero_dte_call_df` on all 15 cycles; the 2k filter changes the top-5 basket in 9 of 15 cycles (mostly APO / CRH / ALAB / LNG entering, low-volume thin names leaving), the 9/4/26 basket is unchanged.
- Weightings (all four computed every run, `WEIGHTINGS`): `EW` = $100k short per name; `MW` = same gross ($100k x n) pro-rata by market cap; `RUNUP` (Zach 9/8/26) = only names whose close-to-close run-up over the `RUNUP_LOOKBACK` trading days into the annc close exceeds 2% (`RUNUP_MIN_PCT`), sized $100k x multiplier, linear from 1x at 2% to 5x at 10%+ (`RUNUP_MAX_PCT`, `RUNUP_MAX_MULT`); `RUNUP_EW` = same gate, flat $100k per name. SPY hedge = resulting gross. `RUNUP_LOOKBACK` was 2 (T-2 -> annc) for the first pass and is now 1 (annc day only, T-1 close -> annc close) -- the one-day gate trades fewer names (31 vs 37 at 2%), catches the eventual add less often (2 vs 3), and scores better (RUNUP_EW +$48k / Sharpe 0.40 / win 73% vs +$42k / 0.38 / 64%). Cycles with no name up > 2% have no RUNUP row.
- Cutoff sweep (9/8/26, top-10 mkt cap, flat $100k, one-day gate): short names up > 2/4/6% on annc day -> +$48k / +$28k / +$4k, 31 / 13 / 8 names; long non-adds down > 2/4/6% on T+1 (variant D) -> +$26k / +$12k / +$9k, but every long row's best cycle is Mar 25 (+$41-43k) and all three are negative ex that cycle. Raising the cutoff shrinks the sample without improving it.
- Variant D (Zach 9/8/26): the reversal trade -- LONG every non-added top-N name at the T+1 close, short $100k SPY per name, exit at the close of the last trading day of that week (T+5 close). `EW` = all non-adds; `DOWN2` = only names down more than 2% (`LONG_DOWN_MIN_PCT`) from the annc close to the T+1 close. Legs carry `side` (+1/-1) and entry/exit date+field so the SPY hedge is timed off the legs; `leg_pnl` is the signed pnl (`short_pnl` kept as alias). Cycles whose T+5 is in the future (9/4/26 until 9/11) are skipped.
- Finding (9/8/26, top-10 mkt cap, 14 cycles): no bounce. D `EW` -$24k (mean -$1.7k, win 43%, Sharpe -0.09); D `DOWN2` +$26k over 11 cycles (win 55%, Sharpe 0.15) but $43k of it is Mar 25 (APP +23%, HOOD +10% off the post-announcement lows); ex that cycle DOWN2 is -$17k. Dec 25 (-$45k EW) and Mar 26 (-$15k) show non-adds kept sliding all week.
- Finding (9/8/26, top-10 mkt cap): RUNUP is the only annc-close weighting that scores well -- variant A T+1 close +$89k over 14 cycles (mean +1.5% of gross, win 64%, Sharpe 0.27), T+1 open +$62k (win 71%, Sharpe 0.39) -- but $70k of it is one cycle (Dec 24: APP +7.4% run-up sized 3.7x) and it is short only 1-5 names per cycle. The 9/4/26 cycle is its worst (-$35k): BE ran +16% into the announcement, got the 5x size, and was the add.
- Horizons: annc close -> T+1 open, annc close -> T+1 close. (Annc -> effective-date close deliberately excluded for now.)
- Cycles with fewer than 10 candidates (all of 2023 + Mar 2024) are flagged `n_lt_top_n` / `*`.
- Today's row is pulled uncached so a partial-day print never lands in the bdh cache.
- **Run standalone as** `python -c "exec(open('short_candidates_t1.py').read())"` -- `my_bdh` spawns a child process, and `python short_candidates_t1.py` makes the spawn re-execute the module (multiprocessing `__main__` guard). In a kernel just run the cells.

**Finding (9/8/26, 15 cycles, 9/4/26 cycle marked at 11am 9/8):**
